{
 "model": "small.en",
 "text": "The security of an encrypted system should not depend on keeping the algorithm secret. An attacker may capture the traffic and study the cipher. Without the key, that knowledge should not be enough to recover the data. Caesar's cipher is the simplest example. Shift each letter by a fixed amount. With so few possible offsets, an attacker can test every one. Key space is too small to provide meaningful security. The Nigma changed substitutions as its rotors advanced. Polish crypt analysts made the first major breakthroughs, and teams at Bletchley Park built on them. The work exploded both the machine structure and the weaknesses in how it was used. As networks expanded, Key Exchange became its own challenge. How did two systems establish a shared secret without first meeting on a trusted channel? Diffie and Hellman's 1976 work offered an answer, but an unauthenticated key exchange still leaves room for an active attacker. The Data Encryption Standard, or DES, became a US federal standard in 1977. Its effective 56-bit key ultimately became too small to resist exhaustive search. In 2001, NIST standardized the Advanced Encryption Standard, or AES, after a public competition to select a stronger successor. Today, evaluating encryption means looking at more than the cipher, how keys are established, how endpoints are authenticated, and how the system is implemented.",
