 "segments": [
  {
   "start": 0.0,
   "end": 5.1,
   "text": "The security of an encrypted system should not depend on keeping the algorithm secret."
  },
  {
   "start": 5.88,
   "end": 8.22,
   "text": "An attacker may capture the traffic and study the cipher."
  },
  {
   "start": 8.86,
   "end": 12.08,
   "text": "Without the key, that knowledge should not be enough to recover the data."
  },
  {
   "start": 14.42,
   "end": 16.1,
   "text": "Caesar's cipher is the simplest example."
  },
  {
   "start": 16.8,
   "end": 18.52,
   "text": "Shift each letter by a fixed amount."
  },
  {
   "start": 19.14,
   "end": 22.14,
   "text": "With so few possible offsets, an attacker can test every one."
  },
  {
   "start": 22.78,
   "end": 25.14,
   "text": "Key space is too small to provide meaningful security."
  },
  {
   "start": 26.34,
   "end": 31.5,
   "text": "The Nigma changed substitutions as its rotors advanced. Polish crypt analysts made the first"
  },
  {
   "start": 31.5,
   "end": 35.86,
   "text": "major breakthroughs, and teams at Bletchley Park built on them. The work exploded both"
  },
  {
   "start": 35.86,
   "end": 38.86,
   "text": "the machine structure and the weaknesses in how it was used."
  },
  {
   "start": 40.26,
   "end": 44.9,
   "text": "As networks expanded, Key Exchange became its own challenge. How did two systems establish"
  },
  {
   "start": 44.9,
   "end": 49.4,
   "text": "a shared secret without first meeting on a trusted channel? Diffie and Hellman's 1976"
  },
  {
   "start": 49.4,
   "end": 54.08,
   "text": "work offered an answer, but an unauthenticated key exchange still leaves room for an active"
  },
  {
   "start": 54.08,
   "end": 54.52,
   "text": "attacker."
  },
  {
   "start": 56.6,
   "end": 62.46,
   "text": "The Data Encryption Standard, or DES, became a US federal standard in 1977. Its effective"
  },
  {
   "start": 62.46,
   "end": 66.52,
   "text": "56-bit key ultimately became too small to resist exhaustive search."
  },
  {
   "start": 68.3,
   "end": 73.72,
   "text": "In 2001, NIST standardized the Advanced Encryption Standard, or AES, after a public competition"
  },
  {
   "start": 73.72,
   "end": 75.32,
   "text": "to select a stronger successor."
  },
  {
   "start": 77.14,
   "end": 81.66,
   "text": "Today, evaluating encryption means looking at more than the cipher, how keys are established,"
  },
  {
   "start": 82.14,
   "end": 84.88,
   "text": "how endpoints are authenticated, and how the system is implemented."
  }
 ]
}